hermes v0.23.0 (on top of every patch before it in package.nix):
background processes of the dashboard survive its restart, and the agent
is told which ones a restart killed.

The dashboard (desktop/web chat backend) ran terminal(background=true)
jobs as plain children, and every dashboard restart (each deploy that
touches the agent's environment) killed them five ways at once: they sat
in the service's cgroup (KillMode=mixed SIGKILLs what is left), the
owner-death supervisor (hermes_cli/process_supervisor.sh) TERMed their
process groups when the backend exited, agent.close killed its session's
jobs on the way out (persist_on_release only spares them for agent
lifecycle sweeps of a live backend), their output went to a pipe the
backend held, and their checkpoint (processes.json) is re-read only by
the messaging gateway (recover_from_checkpoint, gateway/run_startup.py),
which rewrites it from its own empty table at every boot. Nothing
reported the loss: no completion notice, process_manage said "No process
with ID", and the agent learned of it only when someone asked.

New, opt-in `terminal.background_restart_safe` (default false): the
dashboard (web_server.py _on_server_started) turns it on for itself, and
then runs every non-PTY local background job

- in a transient user scope of its own (systemd-run --user --scope
  --unit hermes-bg-<id> --collect, MemoryAccounting only), outside the
  backend's cgroup, not registered with the owner-death supervisor,
- with its output in $HERMES_HOME/logs/process-output/<id>.log and its
  exit status recorded beside it (the managed-worker contract of
  tools/process_registry_output_log.py), so nothing depends on a pipe,
- checkpointed in processes-dashboard.json (the gateway keeps
  processes.json), and spared by agent.close once the backend's SIGTERM
  path started (session_reaper._stop_turns_before_exit, the drain).

A job that ends while the backend shuts down stays in that checkpoint
with its outcome, and this process queues no notice for it (a turn it
started would be cut short too, and the next process reports it). A job
re-adopted from the predecessor is also spared by agent.close later on:
the startup resume reopens its session unattached, and the reaper's close
of that session must not kill the job that just survived the restart
(process_manage kill still stops it). At the next start,
before the startup resume, tools/process_registry_restart.py recover():

- re-adopts every job that still runs or finished meanwhile (the log
  reader collects its recorded exit and sends the normal completion
  notice), through the upstream recover_from_checkpoint,
- marks every other one (a PTY job, a job from before this patch, one
  whose log is gone or that died without recording its exit) finished as
  "terminated by the Hermes restart" with a completion notice (one that
  exited on its own during the shutdown: its real exit), and keeps its
  result for process_manage poll/log,
- remembers both lists per owning session: the startup resume reopens
  the sessions a notice is on its way to, and the first turn of each gets
  one system note listing which jobs kept running and which the restart
  killed ("check what it already did before running it again").

Without the option nothing changes. A PTY job (pty=true) still dies
with the backend; it is reported. The scratch pruner counts the new
checkpoint's PIDs as Hermes-owned, and profile clone/backup skip the file
as they skip processes.json.
